        {
          "term": "random variables",
          "heading": false
        }
      ],
      "extract": "Concentration inequalities are ubiquitous in discrete mathematics and theoretical com- puter science [5, 6]. The most canonical examples are the Chernoff-Hoeffding bounds, which show strong concentration for linear combinations of independent random vari- ables [7, 8]. In some applications, the condition of independence is too restrictive, so weaker notions have been considered [9–11]. Of interest to us is the",
      "extractCut": true
    },
